  "notes": "USAGE:\nAlso written as {指差|ゆびさ}す or {指|ゆび}を{差|さ}す. In Japanese culture, pointing directly at a person is considered impolite, so the expression {人|ひと}を{指|ゆび}さす often carries a negative nuance, implying singling someone out or publicly criticizing them.\n\nGodan verb ({五段動詞|ごだんどうし}), す-ending.\n- Negative: {指|ゆび}ささない\n- Polite: {指|ゆび}さします\n- Te-form: {指|ゆび}さして\n- Past: {指|ゆび}さした\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {人|ひと}を{指|ゆび}さす: to point at a person (often negative)\n- {方向|ほうこう}を{指|ゆび}さす: to point in a direction\n- {後|うし}ろ{指|ゆび}をさされる: to be talked about behind one's back (set expression)",
